[Mono][ppc64le] ELFv2: return small aggregates in registers, extend small vtype arguments

Upstreamable: yes. A portability fix for dotnet/runtime src/mono, not a local
workaround. Paths are relative to src/runtime (dotnet/runtime layout); the
dotnet-core PKGBUILD applies it with `patch -Np1 -d src/runtime`.

What it fixes
=============
1. Mono's ppc64 JIT passed every vtype return value through a hidden pointer in
   r3, the ppc32 SysV rule. On ELFv2 (ppc64le) a native function returning a
   struct of 16 bytes or less, or a homogeneous float aggregate, uses no hidden
   pointer. Mono still put the pointer in r3, so every real argument landed one
   register to the right, and the result was read from memory the callee never
   wrote. mini-ppc.h already defined PPC_RETURN_SMALL_STRUCTS_IN_REGS and
   mini-ppc.c had is_struct_returnable_via_regs(), but get_call_info() never
   used them.

   Seen as: pinta (GirCore 0.7.0) dies at startup with "instance with invalid
   (NULL) class pointer" / "Could not connect to event OnStartup", because
   GirCore declares g_signal_connect_closure_by_id() as returning CULong, a
   one-field struct, so GLib received a stack address as the instance.
   labs/div/lldiv declared with struct returns returned garbage the same way.

2. A vtype of under 8 bytes passed in one register was loaded as a whole
   doubleword, so the bytes after the struct went into the register. The
   64-bit ELF ABI makes the producer sign or zero extend integers narrower
   than 64 bits, and GCC-compiled callees rely on that. Bindings wrap C
   scalars in one-field structs (GirCore's GLib.Quark for GQuark, HarfBuzz's
   codepoint_t, ...), so GLib saw a non-zero detail quark: "signal id '57'
   does not support detail (0)", "Could not connect to event OnActivate".

Rules implemented
=================
Only for native signatures (sig->pinvoke): P/Invoke calls, and the
native-to-managed wrappers of [UnmanagedCallersOnly] methods and marshalled
delegates. Managed-to-managed calls keep returning vtypes by reference.

  Returns (64-bit ELF V2 ABI, "Return Values"):
  - homogeneous float aggregate, up to 8 float or double members (nested
    structs and InlineArray elements flattened; size must equal n * element
    size): members in f1..f8, one per register, a float member as its
    double-precision register image;
  - any other aggregate of at most 16 bytes, including mixed int/float ones:
    its memory image in r3 (bytes 0-7) and r4 (bytes 8-15);
  - anything larger: hidden pointer in r3, unchanged.

  Caller side: the destination address is kept in a volatile local and the
  registers are stored after the call, writing exactly the aggregate's bytes
  (the destination is a native-size stack slot; a whole-doubleword store for a
  3 or 12 byte struct would clobber the neighbouring local).
  Callee side: the value is built in cfg->ret, a stack local
  (ret_var_is_local), and the epilog loads it into r3/r4 or f1..f8.

  Extension (64-bit ELF ABI, integer arguments and return values): a vtype of
  fewer than 8 bytes in one register is loaded from its exact size and extended
  like the scalar it usually wraps: sign-extended when its single field is a
  signed integer filling the struct, zero-extended otherwise. The same applies
  to such a vtype returned in r3 to a native caller.

  Tail calls involving a vtype returned in registers are not supported
  (mono_arch_tailcall_supported).

Also: cpu-ppc64.mdesc lengths for the vcall opcodes grow to cover the store
sequence, and the cpu-ppc64.h genmdesc rule gets the DEPENDS every other
architecture has. Without it, editing cpu-ppc64.mdesc does not regenerate the
header in an existing build tree.

Verification
============
A generated conformance suite: a C library and driver built by GCC 16.1 -O2
-mcpu=power9, and the same cases from .NET (P/Invoke return, return into an
array element between canaries, return through a managed wrapper,
[UnmanagedCallersOnly] callback returning the struct, marshalled-delegate
callback, by-value argument). 42 struct shapes: 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8, 12 and 16
byte integer structs (with and without padding, Pack=1), IntPtr+int, 17-byte
packed and 24-byte structs, mixed int/float and float/double of 8 and 16
bytes, float and double HFAs of 1-9 members, nested HFAs, a nested non-HFA,
an InlineArray float[4]; plus CULong/CLong returns and callbacks, and 1/2/4
byte signed/unsigned wrapper structs checked as the raw 64-bit register on
both the argument and the callback-return path.
The GCC side independently confirms where GCC puts each shape (r3/r4 image,
f1..f8, or hidden pointer) by calling through raw function types.

